# Publish

Ready means a human runs the `Release` workflow (`.github/workflows/release.yml`) and no other step is left. Agents do not run that workflow or `cargo publish`. Agents may run `cargo publish --dry-run`.

`just lint` and `just test` run on every change, publish or not. On the commit that will be published, record them locally. Cloud CI is not the completion condition while runners are limited.

## Order

One pull request says release preparation is complete: what is done, and what is not. A human merges it. The publish review (below) then runs on that `main` commit.

A human runs the `Release` workflow from the Actions tab on `main`, with the version. It runs on the `main` commit of that moment and stops at the first failure:

1. `verify`: `.github/scripts/release-check.sh` (the `Cargo.toml` version is the input, `CHANGELOG.md` has one undated `## [{version}]`, there is no tag `v{version}`, `cargo package --list` matches the whitelist below), then `cargo publish --dry-run`.
2. `test` on 1.88 and stable (the steps of `just lint`, `cargo test`, `cargo doc --no-deps` with warnings denied) and `alloc` (`cargo test --release --test alloc`).
3. `publish`: dates the changelog heading with the day it runs on a release commit (branch `release/v{version}`, child of that `main` commit), creates the annotated tag `v{version}` on it and pushes both, runs `cargo publish` through crates.io Trusted Publishing, creates the GitHub Release on the tag with the changelog section as its notes, and opens a pull request that brings the dated heading to `main`. A human merges that pull request.

Do not create the tag or the Release by hand. If `cargo publish` fails after the tag is pushed, delete the tag and the release branch, fix the cause, and run the workflow again.

## Before the `Release` workflow

All of the following are true for the `main` commit it will run on. A failure in the reader check blocks the workflow.

- `Cargo.toml` `version` is the version to publish. `publish = false` is absent.
- The publish-review skill has been run. No failure remains against `.cursor/rules/reader-docs.mdc` and `.cursor/rules/rust-docs.mdc`.
- `cargo doc --no-deps` reports no broken links.
- `cargo package` builds the package. `cargo package --list` matches the whitelist below.
- `just lint` and `just test` succeeded on that commit (the workflow runs them again).
- crates.io has this repository's `release.yml`, environment `release`, as a Trusted Publisher of `gprx`.
- `cargo publish --dry-run` exits 0 on a clean tree of that commit (the workflow runs it again).

`homepage` is the GitHub repository. `documentation` is `https://docs.rs/gprx`. `[package.metadata.docs.rs]` builds default features, not `bench-internals` or `insert-stages`.

`CHANGELOG.md` (English, [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/)) starts at 0.2.0. The change that publishes a version adds its section: what breaks the previous version, each with how to move, then what is added and changed. On `main` the section heading is undated (`## [0.2.0]`). The `Release` workflow writes the day it publishes, as Keep a Changelog does (`## [0.2.0] - YYYY-MM-DD`), on the release commit; its pull request brings that heading to `main`. Its Verification records `cargo semver-checks` against the previous version, the previous version's tests, examples, benches, and README built and run on that change, and a directory the previous version saved loaded with the same predictions.

## MSRV

The MSRV is 1.88, the same value as `rust-version` in `Cargo.toml`. Edition 2024 does not build on an older compiler. CI runs the steps of `just lint` and `cargo test` on 1.88 and on stable, and the allocation counts (`cargo test --release --test alloc`, the `alloc (release)` job) on stable. 1.88 is the oldest compiler with let chains, which the crate uses; argmin 0.11 needs 1.87 (`is_multiple_of`).

## Coverage

Line coverage is a gate on every pull request and on pushes to `main`.

- Tool: `cargo-llvm-cov`, line coverage.
- What is measured: lines in `src/` hit by `cargo test` with default features. Python, criterion, `bench-internals`, and `insert-stages` are outside the measurement.
- Job: stable only. The 1.88 job does not run it.
- Command: `just coverage`. `just test` (`cargo test` and the release allocation counts) does not start coverage. `tests/alloc.rs` is `test = false`, so coverage does not run it.
- Floor: the integer part of the line-coverage percent measured when the gate is added. CI fails when a later run is under that integer. The integer is recorded on the coverage Issue and in the workflow. It is not written on the roadmap.
- Raising the floor is a new Issue.

## Public contract

A 0.x minor may break the public API. The README says so.

The contract is every item an external crate can name with default features. `Sgpr` and `Svgp` are part of that surface. A `pub` item that no external crate can name is not part of the contract.

`gprx::internals` (features `bench-internals` and `insert-stages`) is not part of the contract. Its rustdoc says so.

## Package files

`Cargo.toml` `include` is a whitelist:

- `src/`
- `examples/`
- `tests/`
- `benches/`
- `compare/goldens/` JSON (integration tests embed these files)
- `README.md` and `README.ja.md`
- `CHANGELOG.md`
- `LICENSE-MIT` and `LICENSE-APACHE`

Root file names in `include` start with `/` (`/README.md`). A pattern without `/` also matches `compare/perf/README.md`.

Do not package `compare/perf`, other Python under `compare/`, `.cursor/`, `.github/`, `docs/`, `rustfmt.toml`, or `clippy.toml`. Links from the README to development docs are absolute GitHub URLs.

`cargo package --list` matches this whitelist.
